      "finalUrl": "https://www.poynter.org/ethics-trust/2026/journalist-creators-media-trust-social-media/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "What journalist-creators know about trust that newsrooms don&#039;t - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "From whiteboard explainers to pop culture references, journalists are using personality to connect with skeptical audiences and make news accessible",
      "evidenceText": "What journalist-creators know about trust that newsrooms don't - Poynter What journalist-creators know about trust that newsrooms don't - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Ethics & Trust Reporting & Editing (Screenshots/biancanobilo) By: Gabrielle Lazor November 11, 2026 For 19-year-old Shelby Moore, the news doesn’t usually come from a headline. It’s most often packaged within the 9:16 aspect ratio of a TikTok or Instagram Reel, where journalists like Eva Pound, an associate booking producer for Punchbowl News, break down politics — sometimes with a pop culture twist. In one series, Pound explained the 2026 midterm elections in the style of reality TV, calling it “Keeping Up With the Kampaigns.” Why the parody? “Because I can promise you that U.S. politics has more money, higher stakes and more drama than any reality TV show. Period,” Pound said in an Instagram Reel that amassed more than 103,000 likes.   View this post on Instagram   A post shared by Eva Pound (@evainthenews) Framing political drama like a reality TV plot “makes it fun and enjoyable but also easy to understand … because lots of people these days don’t look at the news — we’re just all consuming TikTok,” Moore said. The College of William and Mary student is hardly alone. According to the Pew Research Center , 38% of adults ages 18 to 29 regularly get news from news influencers — people with large social media followings who often post about news, politics or social issues. That’s more than four times the share of adults 65 and older (8%). A growing number of journalists are meeting young audiences where they scroll. In doing so, they are discarding legacy media’s traditional stoicism in favor of personality-driven storytelling and pop culture fluency. Above all, they’re leveraging social media algorithms to make news accessible and digestible. Meeting audiences where they scroll “History Uncensored” host and former CNN anchor Bianca Nobilo needs only a couple of minutes and a whiteboard to break down geopolitics for her 269,000 Instagram followers and 112,000 TikTok followers. The whiteboard, a hallmark of Nobilo’s content , emerged organically after several of her friends complained that news stories lacked historical context.   View this post on Instagram   A post shared by Bianca Nobilo (@biancanobilo) “My belief has always been since I’ve been doing this … is that everyone is busy in their own lives,” Nobilo said. “It’s my job to metabolize all the information.” “You can’t expect people to participate properly in democracy if they don’t have the necessary knowledge, but life doesn’t provide time for that knowledge a lot of the time,” she said. Camille McCallum, 32, admits that her screen time is “embarrassingly high.” Social media accounts for roughly 75% of her news diet. With so much news behind paywalls, she finds it easier to follow individual creators. “As I’ve followed news creators, the news comes more directly to me through the algorithm,” McCallum said. McCallum, an influencer and founder of the lifestyle brand Black Woman on a Mission, recently delivered a presentation to political creators on effective get-out-the-vote content. Her biggest takeaways: Young audiences want creators who fill specific knowledge gaps accurately, and they want it in a winning format. She pointed to Pound’s “Kampaign” series as an example. Naturally, surfacing above the algorithmic abyss requires content that is both helpful and entertaining. “Effectiveness comes down to being accurate,” McCallum said. “We’re in an age of mis- and disinformation, so I’m constantly looking for creators I can trust, who cite sources, link articles, and are not just giving their opinions.” The independent trade-off Producing news outside the institutional aegis of legacy media can be a double-edged sword. Independent creators may trade away the safety net and industrial fact-checking power of big newsrooms, but working outside the reputation of an institutional brand can also help them reach audiences who distrust traditional outlets. “I’m frequently interested in how people who are vehemently one side or the other will share my content as proof of their argument,” Nobilo said. (Just check out any one of her comment sections.) Americans’ trust in mass media remains dismal . In a 2025 Gallup poll , seven in 10 Americans said they had “not very much” confidence (36%) or “none at all” (34%) in the news. Among Republicans, trust stood at just 8%. For many viewers, a traditional outlet’s name can be a scarlet letter. “I think, naturally, if you’re an independent personality, that does make you more flexible to move through these groups,” Nobilo said. Without a masthead attached, viewers across party lines can consider a journalist’s reporting without triggering preconceived notions. (Nobilo noted that being a blonde woman covering geopolitics in a predominantly male field brings its own biases.) The converse holds as well. “When you’re part of an institution, you experience the halo effect of that institution to their target audience,” Nobilo said. “You don’t need to earn the trust of somebody who is a CNN viewer … they naturally trust that if CNN has hired you, you meet their standards.” Still, Moore agreed that she’s more inclined to trust an individual journalist than a legacy news organization. Pulling back the curtain The distinctions between terms like “journalist-creator,” “news influencer” and “multimedia reporter” can be blurry. Pew Research defines news influencers as individuals with large followings who regularly post about news, political or social issues. Those personalities can be wholly independent or tied to established newsrooms. But among trained journalists, “influencer” has become sort of a “dirty word,” said Mimi Yates, an investigative reporter for the Daily Mail’s Underground UK team, because “influencing” goes against journalistic principles of integrity. “‘Creator’ is more palatable,” she said. Yates regularly uses Instagram carousels to give the “story behind the story,” showing her reporting process and making her work more transparent. There’s credibility in clarity. Even though Axios senior AI reporter Madison Mills covers a beat that lends itself to jargon, she uses the same language in her videos as she does in her reporting. She said that consistency builds trust with her audience because she doesn’t dumb down stories for social media. Jackie Davalos, a former journalist turned bakery owner, pointed to Mills “as one of the first that I saw really showing her personality — doing her makeup while explaining, like, Disney earnings.” “Even though I hear about these things at work, I was still super engaged,” Davalos said, recalling her time at Bloomberg with Mills. “Not even because she was a friend or a colleague, but because it was very easy to understand and it was accurate.” For Davalos, personality-driven reporting does something legacy media organizations often neglect: it peels back the curtain on the reporting process. “There’s a lot of trust issues, as everyone knows, when it comes to the journalism industry,” Davalos said. “Showing that you’re human, and that there are real barriers at every turn when you’re doing this work — the internal battles, the reporting challenges — kind of helps take the righteousness out of journalism.” “Sometimes it can feel like journalists are talking at you, not necessarily bringing you into the conversation. I don’t think it does us any favors to make this work seem really mysterious.” This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE Tags: audience engagement , digital journalism , Instagram , journalism , journalist-creators , media trust , news influencers , news literacy , Social Media , TikTok Gabrielle Lazor Gabrielle Lazor is a freelance reporter and fellow at the Aspen Institute in Washington, D.C. Her bylines have appeared in outlets including The Washington Post,&hellip; Gabrielle Lazor Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom Congressional investigations could put the administration’s attacks on the press under scrutiny, while bipartisan legislation offers a path to reform November 13, 2026 Angela Fu Opinion | Longtime NBC Sports announcer Dan Hicks, dies suddenly. He was 64 The longtime announcer and host was a staple in sports broadcasting, known for his voice, easy manner and mentorship November 13, 2026 Tom Jones The local newsroom is disappearing. The news could pay the price. As newspapers abandon their buildings, they risk losing the mentoring, institutional knowledge and presence that 