      "id": "310472d00943e79a9804342d6de3c5244cf2761f44c263d35eceadcf0b3586ff",
      "title": "It’s been a great year for investigative reporting. But there’s a catch.",
      "url": "https://www.poynter.org/ethics-trust/2026/scott-pelley-cbs-news-midterm-results",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T18:31:33.655Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.poynter.org/ethics-trust/2026/scott-pelley-cbs-news-midterm-results/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Scott Pelley says CBS has lost its credibility. A post-midterms about-face wouldn’t help. - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "‘It’s not a question of which way the bias leans. The problem is that editorial control was taken from journalists’",
      "evidenceText": "Scott Pelley says CBS has lost its credibility. A post-midterms about-face wouldn’t help. - Poynter Scott Pelley says CBS has lost its credibility. A post-midterms about-face wouldn’t help. - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Ethics & Trust Scott Pelley, then anchor of “CBS Evening News,” poses for a photo in New York on May 15, 2013. (Photo by Charles Sykes/Invision/AP) By: Angela Fu November 12, 2026 As the blue “tsunami” spurred by Democratic midterm wins crashed over the country, some questioned whether the media leaders who tried to appease President Donald Trump would do an about-face. What would happen with, say, CBS News, whose owners have paid millions to Trump and implemented other changes that critics say were made under political pressure from the administration? That question misses the point, according to former “60 Minutes” correspondent Scott Pelley, who was fired in June after he pushed back against editorial changes in his own newsroom. CBS’ credibility, he said, has already been lost. Pelley is set to receive the Poynter Medal for Lifetime Achievement in Journalism this Saturday at Poynter’s Bowtie Ball. There, he will be honored for his more than 50 years in journalism, 37 of which were spent at CBS News, where his roles ranged from war correspondent to “CBS Evening News” anchor to “60 Minutes” correspondent. His career at “60 Minutes” — whose staff he had called “family” — ended not long after Skydance acquired CBS’ owner Paramount and installed The Free Press founder Bari Weiss as editor-in-chief. What followed was a string of firings and departures at “60 Minutes,” where journalists have alleged that Weiss interfered with their editorial independence. (Weiss has denied this.) Things came to a head June 1 when Pelley confronted “60 Minutes” executive producer Nick Bilton at a staff meeting. Pelley told Bilton, who was hired by Weiss and had never worked in television, that he had “slender qualifications.” Weiss, Pelley said, had “no qualifications” and was “murdering” “60 Minutes.” The next day, Pelley was fired. Ahead of the Bowtie Ball, Pelley agreed to answer several questions via email about the direction of CBS News, Skydance and his own career. Angela Fu: You have said that CBS in the past two years has tried to curry favor with the Trump administration. Do you see this changing, given the results of the midterms? Scott Pelley : To me, it’s not a question of which way the bias leans. The problem is that editorial control was taken from journalists trained in our principles and given to corporate executives and ideologues with an agenda. It doesn’t matter which agenda is imposed; independence and credibility are lost. Fu: You’ve now had several months to reflect on your time at “60 Minutes” and your decision to confront Nick Bilton. Looking back, would you do anything differently? Pelley : No. I stood up for my colleagues. I stood up for integrity. I stand up for them today. If anything, the circumstances are more grave. With the closing of the Warner merger, Skydance is partly owned by the Saudi sovereign wealth fund. The fund is chaired by Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, the kingdom’s de facto leader, who, according to U.S. intelligence, approved the operation that killed and dismembered Washington Post columnist Jamal Khashoggi. I cannot reconcile partial ownership of CBS News and CNN with the murder of a journalist. For this reason alone, I could not have remained at CBS News. Fu: In the wake of the Paramount Skydance-Warner Bros. merger, what is your advice to the journalists at CNN? What should they watch for? Pelley : They should watch for ownership reneging on its promise of independence. They should watch for pressure to kill stories the ownership doesn’t like. They should watch for efforts to inject bias into their reporting. They should watch for the firing of colleagues who stand up for journalistic values. In our experience, the attack on “60 Minutes” was not subtle. Fu: You went from working for one of the most high-profile legacy newsrooms in the country to being an independent journalist. What was that transition like? Did anything surprise you? Pelley : I’m surprised by how much I am enjoying the role of an independent journalist. In my media company, I am able to lead my team with integrity and return to the values I was taught in nearly 40 years at CBS News. I’m engaged in reporting, writing, documentary production and podcast development. I’m speaking all over the country on the indispensable role of press freedom in a democracy, and I’m lecturing at Columbia Journalism School. The opportunities are endless. Fu: What are your go-to news sources right now? What are you reading and/or watching? Pelley : I am a little old-fashioned in this. Every morning I read The New York Times, The Washington Post and The Wall Street Journal. I’ve frequently admired the Journal’s editorial page for insight into Trump administration policy. I’m watching some truly great documentaries, and I’m a fan of PBS’s “NewsHour” and “Frontline.” Fu: You’ve been very critical of CBS’ editorial decisions these past two years. Zooming out, how would you evaluate the media’s coverage of Donald Trump and key issues today? What is the industry overall doing well, or what is it missing in its coverage? Pelley : The investigative reporting into corruption in the Trump administration has been excellent, and some of it may, in time, become legendary. I am not seeing nearly enough coverage of the war with Iran, but that is largely due to Pentagon leaders who are hostile to the public’s right to know. As a profession, we can never do too much explanatory reporting on the unregulated frontiers of AI. There is great public service to be done by illuminating a technology that will transform civilization. This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE Tags: 60 Minutes , bari weiss , Bowtie Ball , CBS , CBS News , CBS. 60 Minutes , front page highlights , Nick Bilton , Paramount , Paramount Skydance , Paramount-Warner merger , Poynter Institute Bowtie Ball , Scott Pelley , Skydance Angela Fu Angela Fu is a reporter for Poynter. She can be reached at afu@poynter.org, on Signal at angelafu.74, on Bluesky @angelanfu.bsky.social and on Twitter @angelanfu. Angela Fu Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom Congressional investigations could put the administration’s attacks on the press under scrutiny, while bipartisan legislation offers a path to reform November 13, 2026 Angela Fu Opinion | Longtime NBC Sports announcer Dan Hicks, dies suddenly. He was 64 The longtime announcer and host was a staple in sports broadcasting, known for his voice, easy manner and mentorship November 13, 2026 Tom Jones The local newsroom is disappearing. The news could pay the price. As newspapers abandon their buildings, they risk losing the mentoring, institutional knowledge and presence that help make good reporting possible November 13, 2026 Jackie Spinner Scott Pelley, then anchor of “CBS Evening News,” poses for a photo in New York on May 15, 2013. (Photo by Charles Sykes/Invision/AP) Scott Pelley says CBS has lost its credibility. A post-midterms about-face wouldn’t help. ‘It’s not a question of which way the bias leans. The problem is that editorial control was taken from journalists’ November 12, 2026 Angela Fu Opinion | Face the changes: Two top leaders are leaving CBS News’ ‘Face the Nation’ The departures of two veteran editorial leaders give Bari Weiss an opening to reshape the influential Sunday political news program November 12, 2026 Tom Jones Back to News Comments Cancel reply You must be logged in to post a comment. This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed. Become a Poynter Member Don't miss out on a year of learning. JOIN TODAY Already a Member? Log in Start your day informed and inspired. Subscribe Get the Poynter newsletter that's right for you. Media Jobs News Editor Director of Development, Word In Black Director of Advertising DIGITAL SALES STRATEGIST BROADCAST JOURNALIST: ARTS & CULTURE Deputy Managing Editor- News Director of Technology Chair and Professor of the Practice Publisher and Chief Executive Officer - Connecticut Mirror / CT Mirror News Editor, The Stranger - Seattle, WA (98122) Search Media Jobs Advertise a job &times; This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE",
