      "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt Some of the biggest scoops of the 2026 midterms came from national outlets, raising questions about the state of local investigative reporting Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . Good afternoon!  Have you caught this week&rsquo;s &ldquo;Poynter Report Podcast&rdquo; yet? CNN&rsquo;s John King, of Magic Wall fame, spoke to host Tom Jones about last week&rsquo;s historic midterm elections. And he didn&rsquo;t hold back on the superlatives, calling the results &ldquo;stunning&rdquo; and saying MAGA and Trumpism &ldquo;got whooped.&rdquo; He also broke down what goes into his Magic Wall marathon ballet. Check it out. Here&rsquo;s what else caught my attention this week. National news outlets landed the biggest stories of the 2026 campaign. That&rsquo;s a problem. The scoops were ceaseless this election season. From the pile of allegations against Graham Platner in Maine to the pileup of Dan Sullivans in Alaska. From abuse allegations involving an Ohio congressman to a Kansas senator aggressively pursuing former patients over unpaid medical bills. It was a hell of a year for investigative reporting. But there was something unusual about all those scoops. Many came from national news outlets, not the local reporters who cover these politicians every day. In a column for Poynter, veteran media reporter Paul Farhi argues that this says as much about the state of local news as it does about the strength of national journalism. National journalists should absolutely be doing this work, but the local newsrooms that once broke these stories increasingly lack the resources to do it themselves. Read the story ▸ Here&rsquo;s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom The press has been under near-constant attack since Donald Trump began his second term. Poynter has documented more than 100 actions by Trump and the federal government targeting journalists and news organizations. Now that Democrats have won control of both chambers of Congress, can they do anything to stop it? Poynter&rsquo;s Angela Fu spoke with press freedom advocates about what the new Congress could accomplish. They see opportunities to investigate officials who have targeted the press, strengthen protections for journalists and push for greater government transparency. But with Trump still holding veto power, how much can Democrats actually get done? Read the story ▸ Scott Pelley says CBS has lost its credibility. A post-midterms about-face wouldn&rsquo;t help. Of course, not every threat to press freedom comes from the government. Sometimes the pressure comes from the people who own the newsrooms. Just ask Scott Pelley, who was fired from &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; earlier this year after publicly challenging the new leadership at CBS News. In an interview with Angela Fu, Pelley argues that the network sacrificed its credibility by putting editorial decisions in the hands of corporate executives rather than journalists. And he doesn&rsquo;t think a change in political fortunes will fix that. Pelley will receive Poynter&rsquo;s Medal for Lifetime Achievement in Journalism at our Bowtie Ball on Saturday. Read the story ▸ Behind the scenes at Poynter Our most-read story since my last newsletter was &ldquo;As Democrats swept the midterms, news anchors struggled to find the words.&rdquo; I&rsquo;ve been Poynter&rsquo;s managing editor since March 2020, just a few days before the pandemic struck. We&rsquo;ve covered four major election nights since then, and though they were all pretty remarkable, none of them was quite like this one. We kept hearing one word in news coverage all night. See what everybody was reading ▸ Pop quiz My colleague Loreben Tuquero at PolitiFact looked back at election spending and found that campaigns and their supporters spent $187 million on &hellip; secretly paying influencers to share their messages travel to Florida Gators games promoting AI-generated ads Kalshi bets Think you know? Check your answer ▸ Please help us shape AI policy guidance When Poynter launched our AI guidelines in 2024, we relied on insights from newsrooms, editors and reporters. As the technology has evolved, we've continued updating that guidance. But we know useful AI policies can't be static, and we need to keep hearing from the people using these tools in their daily work. As we approach 2027, we are asking for your input to help us update and revise our AI policy guidance. Please be part of the process by completing this short 10-minute survey to help us understand where and how newsrooms are using AI. Your confidential response will be used to help guide and form our policy updates. More from Poynter this week What journalist-creators know about trust that newsrooms don&rsquo;t (Gabrielle Lazor) In a West Virginia county of 6,540, accountability journalism gets personal (Kelly McBride) After midterm elections, Trump said he was &lsquo;too tough&rsquo; on immigration. Here&rsquo;s what he&rsquo;s done so far (Amy Sherman and Maria Brice&ntilde;o for PolitiFact) TikTok : AI companies like Anthropic, Meta and OpenAI are buying up tons of old books, tearing off the spines and feeding their data into large language models. (MediaWise) Instagram : No, we will not be sending you the questions in advance. Or the draft, for that matter. More Poynter: Check out &ldquo;The Poynter Report Podcast&rdquo; on Spotify , Apple Podcasts , Amazon Music or YouTube . Subscribe to our other newsletters: The Poynter Report , Local Edition , Factually , the MediaWise Newsletter and Poynter Pro Weekly . Follow us on LinkedIn and Instagram . Ren LaForme Managing Editor The Poynter Institute  @itsren Please consider donating some of the $187 million you were otherwise going to spend on Kalshi bets to support Poynter&rsquo;s journalism GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or unsubscribe from all Poynter emails .",
