      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "How Dan Hicks helped define an era of greatness",
      "pageDescription": "Dan Hicks contributed so much to the sports broadcasting world, but the highlights were Tiger Woods&#039; and Michael Phelps&#039; biggest moments.",
      "evidenceText": "How Dan Hicks helped define an era of greatness Skip to content Credit: Reuters/NBC, edit via Liam McGuire By Matt Yoder on 11/12/2026 11/12/2026 Less AI and more AA in your search results. Dan Hicks was a constant presence at some of the biggest sporting events in the world. After his sudden passing at the age of 64 following an acute illness, it’s almost unfathomable to imagine them without him. Hicks was a longtime fixture at NBC Sports, where he worked for well over 30 years. During that time, he called a number of marquee sporting events. The list includes, but is not limited to, NFL playoff games, Notre Dame football, the NBA on NBC, PGA Tour events and majors, and both the Summer and Winter Olympics. When you think of Dan Hicks as a broadcaster, the word “professional” immediately comes to mind. Hicks wasn’t one to overshadow any broadcast with his own personality — the game and the athletes on display were always the star. He hardly ever had a major moment go sideways. But if you ever tuned in to one of his broadcasts, you knew you were going to get a top-level performance because that’s what he did week in, week out, for decades. The scores of tributes from Hicks’ colleagues across the sports media show how respected he was by those inside the industry, both for his professionalism on the air and his life away from the cameras. But when looking back on Hicks’ impact as a broadcaster, it’s fitting that he will always be associated with two of the greatest athletes of the 2000s, as he provided commentary for so many famous triumphs of Michael Phelps and Tiger Woods. Dan Hicks called plenty of memorable games and moments at NBC Sports ranging from one of the greatest comebacks in NFL playoff history to countless gold medal events, major golf championships, and Ryder Cups. But decades from now, you will still hear his voice chronicling the otherworldly achievements of Woods and Phelps. “Expect anything different?” Perhaps the defining call of Hicks’ career came at the 2008 US Open at Torrey Pines . A clearly hobbled Tiger Woods was chasing his third US Open championship while battling a wrecked knee and a broken leg. On the 72nd hole, he needed to make a 12-foot birdie putt to force an 18-hole playoff with veteran pro Rocco Mediate. Woods would go on to win the playoff and his third US Open the next day. As the putt curled in, Hicks calmly and confidently delivered one of the most famous calls in golf history, “Expect anything different?” Dan Hicks was a master of unscripted play-by-play, voicing many of golf’s most famous moments over the last 30 years. Jimmy Roberts remembers his iconic call of Tiger Woods’ putt at the 2008 U.S. Open: “Expect anything different?” https://t.co/sIzzzJDt89 — Golf Channel (@GolfChannel) November 12, 2026 It was perfect for the moment because it wasn’t the most poetic call. It simply pointed to the absurd brilliance of Tiger Woods for the millions watching at home to marvel at. How could you not expect him to make that putt ? It was a message that we all were indeed watching a once-in-a-generation athlete simply willing himself to victory. It was prototypical Dan Hicks in that the moment wasn’t about his description; it was about lifting up greatness. Golf isn’t a sport that lends itself to many unforgettable calls because there’s rarely any live narration of the ball in play. You’re just watching to see where a ball ends up or if it falls into a hole in the ground. But Hicks’ call will live alongside “Yes sir!” “In your life!” and “Better than most!” as one of the most iconic in its history. “Unbelievable at the end!” Along with golf, Dan Hicks will be most fondly remembered for being the voice of swimming at the Summer Olympics. His time in the broadcast booth just so happened to correspond with the most decorated athlete in the history of the games, Michael Phelps. Hicks was there for all of Phelps’ record 23 gold medals and several world records. While Phelps’ heroics spanned four Olympic games, his peak also came in 2008 at the Beijing Olympics. Phelps broke Mark Spitz’s famous record by winning a combined eight gold medals in individual and team events. But the great irony is that Phelps’ most famous medal, and Hicks’ most famous call, came thanks to Team USA teammate Jason Lezak in the 4×100-meter freestyle relay . Phelps swam the first leg for the Americans, but by the time Jason Lezak entered the pool for the anchor leg, he was trailing the French team by a substantial margin. However, Lezak somehow swam a 46.06 split (almost 1.5 seconds faster than Phelps) and won in thrilling fashion. Dan Hicks’ call of the Michael Phelps-Jason Lezak relay from the 2008 Olympics will long live as one of the greatest ever. Rest in peace to a legend. https://t.co/6CZfqmfsU1 — Mark Harris (@itismarkharris) November 12, 2026 Forget Olympic history; that race and relay leg will stand the test of time as one of the most exciting moments in all of modern-day sports. And it got the treatment it deserved from the usually understated Hicks, who rose to the occasion and screamed in excitement and disbelief while nailing the call and even referencing Phelps’ quest for history staying alive. Most sports broadcasters can go an entire career without being privileged to call anything like that. But Dan Hicks got to call the peak performances of legendary figures at their very best within just a couple of months of each other. Of course, Hicks provided the soundtrack for many other stars from the golf course to the gridiron and from the warm waters of the Olympic swimming pool to the snowy mountains of alpine skiing. There was Katie Ledecky, Rory McIlroy, Andrew Luck, Lindsey Vonn, and many others. But many years from now, those two moments and those two calls will still be replayed for future generations to enjoy and be in awe. It was both the best of sports and the best of sports broadcasting. And that association is what fans will remember and appreciate most about Dan Hicks. He never needed to be the biggest personality in the broadcast booth, but when the biggest moments in sports happened, you were glad he was there to document them. About Matt Yoder Matt writes the Awful Announcing 'The A Block' newsletter every Wednesday. Subscribe here . View all posts by Matt Yoder Follow on Twitter Related Content Corned beef, Cheerwine, and carving stations: Ranking every NHL team’s media meals Where have you gone, Lee Corso? College football fans falling out of love with Saturday pregame shows Five players who deserved to win the Heisman Trophy but didn’t Why Joe Buck and Troy Aikman is the most painful breakup in sports media history Recent Posts Washington Commanders quarterback Sam Hartman will follow in the footsteps of Jesse Palmer in starring as 'The Bachelor.' Brett Yormark is leaving the Big 12 to run a new venture for Redbird Capital built around their ownership of AC Milan. The NHL is ready to come to the negotiating table to renew its rights with ESPN and TNT at an increase over its current deal. Credit: ‘Run It Back’ on FanDuel TV \"You gotta be kidding me!\" 'When We Were Young and Fearless' examines how Kevin Durant, Russell Westbrook, and James Harden changed the NBA. Sidebar Popular Posts The 76ers are either building a superteam or creating a superproblem Is a sports media blue wave coming, too? Recent Posts Washington Commanders quarterback Sam Hartman will follow in the footsteps of Jesse Palmer in starring as 'The Bachelor.' Brett Yormark is leaving the Big 12 to run a new venture for Redbird Capital built around their ownership of AC Milan. The NHL is ready to come to the negotiating table to renew its rights with ESPN and TNT at an increase over its current deal. Credit: ‘Run It Back’ on FanDuel TV \"You gotta be kidding me!\" 'When We Were Young and Fearless' examines how Kevin Durant, Russell Westbrook, and James Harden changed the NBA.",
