      "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt The longtime announcer and host was a staple in sports broadcasting, known for his voice, easy manner and mentorship Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION Longtime NBC Sports announcer Dan Hicks, dies suddenly. He was 64 NBC Sports announcer Dan Hicks. (Photo: NBC Sports) Horrible news from the sports world. NBC Sports play-by-play voice Dan Hicks has died after what his family said was an acute illness. Hicks was 64. The husband of longtime ESPN anchor Hannah Storm, Hicks was NBC&rsquo;s lead play-by-play voice on its PGA Tour coverage since 2000, In addition, he has covered 16 Olympics and was the play-by-play announcer on the network&rsquo;s coverage of Notre Dame football. In fact, according to a schedule released by NBC Sports earlier this week, Hicks was set to call this Saturday&rsquo;s Notre Dame against Boston College. Storm said in a statement , &ldquo;Our family is heartbroken by the sudden and unexpected passing of Dan following an acute illness. He was a devoted husband of over 30 years, loving father to our three daughters, and cherished friend to so many. His loss is immeasurable.&rdquo; She continued, &ldquo;Dan was truly one of a kind and, quite simply, the person everyone wanted to be around. When he walked into a room, he brought the energy, the laughter and the people together. He was endlessly kind, thoughtful and funny, and he made everyone feel like they mattered. Our three daughters and I are heartbroken. We are grateful for the love and support surrounding our family during this incredibly difficult time. These are simply no words to express how much he will be missed.&rdquo; The news rocked the sports media world. Hicks was not only a smooth, professional and superb announcer, but he had a reputation for being easy to work with and someone who served as a mentor to younger broadcasters. NBC and Golf Channel analyst Roger Maltbie said on the air Thursday, &ldquo;This is not losing a colleague and a nice guy; this is losing a pal.&rdquo; Versant CEO Mark Lazarus said in a statement, &ldquo;I am heartbroken by the sudden loss of our friend Dan Hicks. For decades, I had the privilege of working alongside Dan, but our friendship extended far beyond NBC Sports. Dan was one of the very best at what he did, but what I will remember most is the person he was: a loyal friend and a devoted husband and father. My family and I are thinking of Hannah, their children and the entire Hicks family. We will miss him.&rdquo; Storm&rsquo;s boss, ESPN chairman Jimmy Pitaro, said in a statement: &ldquo;We are deeply saddened by the sudden passing of Dan Hicks, a respected voice in sports broadcasting and husband of ESPN anchor Hannah Storm. Hannah is a cherished member of the ESPN family, and our hearts are with her, their three daughters and all who knew and loved Dan, including his NBC colleagues, during this incredibly difficult time. We extend our deepest condolences and stand alongside Hannah and her family.&rdquo; Hicks could call practically any sport, but he was best known for his golf announcing. Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Drew Lerner wrote , &ldquo;Hicks called any number of legendary moments throughout his career. Among them was his memorable call on the 72nd hole of the 2008 U.S. Open, when Tiger Woods rolled in a 12-foot birdie putt to tie Rocco Mediate and force a playoff the next day. &lsquo;Expect anything different!?&rsquo; Hicks bellowed in a call that would live on forever.&rdquo; The Athletic&rsquo;s Brody Miller wrote , &ldquo;Hicks understood how to make golf about the moment, and now those moments will feel a little more empty.&rdquo; Hicks&rsquo; Olympic coverage was highlighted by calling Michael Phelps&rsquo; legendary run of eight gold medals in the 2008 Summer Games. The Athletic&rsquo;s Andrew Marchand wrote , &ldquo;His broadcasts were consistently strong and never about him. In an age of schtick, he delivered the goods with rare versatility, where his bosses could ask him to call play-by-play on a whim or host. Hicks&rsquo; ability to host and call the action made him perfect for golf, and he became synonymous with NBC&rsquo;s coverage.&rdquo; Marchand added, &ldquo;It is an extremely sad day for the golf, Olympic and football communities with the news of Hicks&rsquo; passing. No words can make anything right for Storm and their three daughters. One day, maybe, they can find a little solace in the fact that Hicks left behind quite a legacy.&rdquo; And Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Matt Yoder put it perfectly when he wrote , &ldquo;When you think of Dan Hicks as a broadcaster, the word &ldquo;professional&rdquo; immediately comes to mind. Hicks wasn&rsquo;t one to overshadow any broadcast with his own personality &mdash; the game and the athletes on display were always the star. He hardly ever had a major moment go sideways. But if you ever tuned in to one of his broadcasts, you knew you were going to get a top-level performance because that&rsquo;s what he did week in, week out, for decades.&rdquo; Poynter honors Pelley Poynter will honor longtime former &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; correspondent Scott Pelley with the Poynter Medal for Lifetime Achievement in Journalism this Saturday at Poynter&rsquo;s Bowtie Ball in Tampa. Ahead of this weekend&rsquo;s festivities, Pelley spoke with my colleague Angela Fu about a number of topics, including his leaving &ldquo;60 Minutes,&rdquo; the Skydance-Warner Bros Discover merger, his current media diet and what life is like as an independent journalist. Fu asked Pelley about his decision to confront new &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; executive producer Nick Bolton, a confrontation that led to Pelley&rsquo;s dismissal from the program, and, looking back, if he would have done anything differently. Pelley said, &ldquo;No. I stood up for my colleagues. I stood up for integrity. I stand up for them today. If anything, the circumstances are more grave. With the closing of the Warner merger, Skydance is partly owned by the Saudi sovereign wealth fund. The fund is chaired by Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, the kingdom&rsquo;s de facto leader, who, according to U.S. intelligence, approved the operation that killed and dismembered Washington Post columnist Jamal Khashoggi. I cannot reconcile partial ownership of CBS News and CNN with the murder of a journalist. For this reason alone, I could not have remained at CBS News.&rdquo; Shooter story A gunman in Washington, D.C. shot five people on Thursday, killing one, before dying of a self-inflicted gunshot wound. Police identified the shooter as Matt Lambert, 55. Authorities said he had been in an ongoing dispute with his condo association over maintenance issues and mold. The Washington Post&rsquo;s Danielle Paquette, Douglas MacMillan, Paul Schwartzman and Aaron Schaffer have more details here . Now check out this story. The Washington Sun&rsquo;s Michael Brice-Saddler wrote &ldquo;What the Logan Circle Shooter Told Me.&rdquo; Brice-Saddler wrote, &ldquo;Lambert reached out to me in late 2025 when I was a reporter at The Washington Post, pitching a story about how his million-dollar Logan Circle penthouse had been rendered unlivable. I continued reporting the story into the new year, but the article was abandoned when I was laid off from the Post in early 2026.&rdquo; Media news, tidbits and interesting links for your weekend review Washington Post executive editor Matt Murray tells AdWeek&rsquo;s Mark Stenberg , &ldquo;We had years of losses, and steepening losses. That&rsquo;s the truth. We&rsquo;re a private company and don&rsquo;t share the figures, but it was hundreds and hundreds of millions of dollars. Now we&rsquo;re within striking distance of break-even, and I expect next year will be our best year in seven years.&rdquo; The Associated Press&rsquo; Fernanda Pesce with &ldquo;In Mexico, journalists face threats from attackers &mdash; and from the system meant to protect them, too.&rdquo; The Atlantic&rsquo;s Adam Kirsch with &ldquo;The Last Trumpist Standing.&rdquo; One day after a soldier guarding the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier admonished Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and his entourage after someone in the group laughed loudly, Hegseth awarded the soldier with a commendation medal. Here&rsquo;s more from The Washington Post&rsquo;s Alex Horton and Tara Copp. Great to see the byline of former Washington Post media reporter Paul Farhi. He writes for Poynter: &ldquo;National news outlets landed the biggest stories of the 2026 campaign. That&rsquo;s a problem.&rdquo; Also for Poynter (and originally published in the Student Press Report , a national news desk covering student media and journalism education in higher ed), it&rsquo;s Rhea Patney with &ldquo;How a Nashville newspaper is turning college students into editors and teenagers into reporters.&rdquo; Rolling Stone&rsquo;s David Fear scores a big interview with the co-directors of the Elizabeth Holmes documentary &mdash; Nathan Fielder and Lance Oppenheim &mdash; as well as actress Amanda Seyfried, who also has a major impact on the documentary. It&rsquo;s all here in &ldquo;The &lsquo;You Can See Everything&rsquo; Interview You&rsquo;ve Been Waiting For.&rdquo; Media fight! Fox Sports 1&rsquo;s Nick Wright and conservative commentator Clay Travis are bickering over Wright&rsquo;s claim that Travis&rsquo; once admitted to fabricating his political beliefs for audience engagement. Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Megan Armstrong has the details here . There&rsquo;s more. Here&rsquo;s Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Brandon Contes with &ldquo;Nick Wright willing to wager $1 million to prove Clay Travis is a political grifter.&rdquo; Bloomberg&rsquo;s Ashley Carman with &ldquo;Bill Simmons canceled ChatGPT partnership after fan backlash.&rdquo; On Aug. 20, a hockey player for Arizona State University collapsed during an offseason workout. The player&rsquo;s attorney says the 20-year-old sophomore suffered exertional heat stroke and remains on life support with no chance of recovery. The coach of the team has since been fired. Here&rsquo;s The Athletic&rsquo;s Katie Strang, Adam Kilgore and Nathan Fenno with &ldquo;A crisis mismanaged: Inside 57 days of tragedy and turmoil at Arizona State.&rdquo; Be sure to check out the latest episode of &ldquo;The Poynter Report Podcast&rdquo; with my guest, CNN&rsquo;s John King. We talk about the midterm elections and what lies ahead for the rest of Donald Trump&rsquo;s presidency. More resources for journalists Protect your newsroom&rsquo;s digital journalism with Today&rsquo;s News for Tomorrow , a free program offering expert training, professional archiving tools and ongoing support to preserve local news for future generations . Apply by Dec. 11. Build practical AI skills while learning to use the technology responsibly. Explore AI tools, ethics, misinformation and bias, learn how to apply AI to your everyday work, and earn a Poynter certificate. Complete the core course in just 4-5 hours, anytime and at your own pace. Protect your sources, your audience and yourself with specialized training on trauma, suicide, addiction and mental health disparities with The Mental Health Reporting Project   &mdash; developed in partnership with The Carter Center. Free due to the generous support of the Brain & Behavior Research Foundation. Have feedback or a tip? Email Poynter senior media writer Tom Jones at tjones@poynter.org . The Poynter Report is your daily dive into the world of media, packed with the latest news and insights. Get it delivered to your inbox Monday through Friday by signing up here . And don&rsquo;t forget to tune into our biweekly podcast for even more. Support the journalism that keeps you informed. GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or unsubscribe from all Poynter emails .",
