      "pageTitle": "Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "Congressional investigations could put the administration’s attacks on the press under scrutiny, while bipartisan legislation offers a path to reform",
      "evidenceText": "Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom - Poynter Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Reporting & Editing Newly-elected members of the 120th Congress gather for a group photo on the steps of the House of Representatives at the Capitol in Washington, Thursday, Nov. 12, 2026. (AP Photo/J. Scott Applewhite) By: Angela Fu November 13, 2026 Less than 48 hours after it became apparent that Democrats would take both chambers of Congress, Sen. Richard Blumenthal (D-Conn.) emailed the Federal Communications Commission, demanding records for an investigation into attacks on the First Amendment. That investigation, led by Democratic senators on a Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs subcommittee, was looking into how President Donald Trump’s and the FCC’s actions “erode and undermine freedom of speech and freedom of the press and bear on the laws governing media consolidation,” Blumenthal wrote in the email. It was a preview of the levers a Democrat-controlled Congress might pull next year to protect press freedom, which has been under severe attack by the second Trump administration. Armed with the power of oversight and investigation, Congress next term could push back against federal agencies that have gone after journalists and their First Amendment rights. Press freedom advocates are also eyeing several key pieces of legislation to support journalists that have received bipartisan support in the past. “The midterms and the results really create an opportunity to strengthen freedom of the press,” said José Zamora, the regional director for the Americas at the Committee to Protect Journalists. “What matters now is whether Congress turns that opportunity into action.” Critics have derided the current Republican-controlled Congress as unproductive. That could change come Jan. 3. The blue “tsunami” has buoyed Democrats, who appear eager to capitalize on the substantial majorities they gained in both chambers, which may increase even further as races continue to be called. On Election Night, Rep. Robert Garcia (D-Calif.), who will chair the House Oversight Committee next year, posted on X, “LAWYER UP.” Their priorities for the next two years are still coming into focus, and press freedom advocates have already set up calls with legislators to lobby for specific issues. Freedom of the Press Foundation chief of advocacy Seth Stern said that securing a free press can help Congress tackle other issues. For example, those who support healthcare legislation need a press that is able to expose abuses and inefficiencies in the current system. “I don’t think press freedom needs to come at the expense of other congressional priorities,” Stern said. “If anything, it’s complementary to all of those other priorities. A free and unencumbered press is only going to help reformers implement reforms because it helps them expose the needs for those reforms.” Investigations and oversight Congress members have a long list of people and issues they want to investigate. At the top of press freedom advocates’ list is FCC Chairman Brendan Carr. At least two groups have outright called for Carr’s impeachment. Under his leadership, the FCC — which is supposed to be independent — has opened investigations into numerous outlets that have faced criticism from conservatives. Egged on by Trump, the FCC’s dispute with ABC has been particularly contentious. ABC filed a lawsuit in August accusing the FCC of weaponizing its regulatory powers to censor and punish ABC for its speech. Carr himself has made thinly veiled threats against outlets, warning that the FCC can revoke their broadcast licenses. The FCC has also repealed a key broadcast ownership cap, paving the way for increasing media consolidation. Its oversight over the transfer of broadcast licenses means that companies pursuing mergers must get approval from the FCC. That power, critics say, has led to companies like Skydance — which is the result of two successive mergers between CBS-parent Paramount, Skydance Media and CNN-parent Warner Bros. Discovery — taking actions to curry favor with Trump. On Tuesday, during a Senate committee hearing on the nomination of Danielle Thumann to the FCC, Sen. Ben Ray Luján (D-N.M.) had a warning for Carr about what he can expect come January. “Mr. Carr, if you’re listening now, know you’re going to be in this body once a month, if not more, and in the House of Representatives as well, answering questions, and you don’t have the protection of the gavel any longer.” Other major administration figures that Congress could investigate and question are Attorney General Todd Blanche and Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth, said Clayton Weimers, the executive director of Reporters Without Borders’ North American branch. Weimers said lawmakers should question Blanche about the FBI’s January raid of a Washington Post reporter’s home. Meanwhile, Hegseth has forced journalists out of the Pentagon, censored information about the war in Iran and is currently trying to turn the editorially independent Stars and Stripes newspaper into a mouthpiece for the Pentagon, Weimers said. Hegseth narrowly survived an impeachment effort Tuesday when the House voted 211 to 204 to refer eight articles of impeachment to committee. Only one Republican voted with Democrats. But next year, Democrats will have a majority if they choose to refile impeachment charges. If Congress decides to pursue investigations into the Trump administration next session, they should invest in reforming transparency laws like the Freedom of Information Act, said Lauren Harper, the Freedom of the Press Foundation’s Daniel Ellsberg Chain on Government Secrecy. FOIA processing times have been an issue for multiple administrations, but the situation has worsened under Trump. The Department of Government Efficiency’s cuts last year led to layoffs of FOIA staff at various agencies. Others have been fired for lawfully releasing documents over the objections of agency leaders. “It’s usually the same offices that play a role in responding to requests for information for both FOIA and for Congress,” Harper said. “So the capability, the software, the resources, the independence of FOIA offices is going to play a role with congressional inquiries.” Actions Congress could take include improving the statutory language of FOIA, funding FOIA offices and imposing financial penalties for officials or agencies who obstruct the release of documents, Harper said. Given the mass detentions of migrants initiated by the Department of Homeland Security and ICE over the past two years, Congress could also extend FOIA to include private prisons and detention facilities. FOIA has historically received bipartisan support, which would be important if Trump decides to veto any legislation Congress puts forth. Harper added that the near-unanimous support for the Epstein Files Transparency Act , which required the release of unclassified records related to convicted sex offender Jeffrey Epstein, also suggests that lawmakers from both sides of the aisle are interested in transparency legislation. “What Congress should do is basically take a lesson from what lawmakers did after Watergate,” Harper said. “After the Nixon administration and his resignation, Congress passed a bunch of transparency laws that really laid the foundation for modern oversight. … FOIA was passed in 1966, but it didn’t get real teeth in enforcement mechanism until 1974 after Watergate. “Watergate was the reason why Congress felt the need to actually do that.” Legislation Though Democrats will not have a veto-proof majority next year, press freedom advocates still see opportunity for Congress to advance legislation protecting journalists. A number of potential bills that have received bipartisan support in the past could be reintroduced. Their support may even grow as Republicans try to distance themselves from the unpopular policies and actions of the Trump administration that led to last week’s midterm results, Stern said. “I think a clear message was sent that the Trump administration’s abuses need to be reined in and stopped from happening again,” Stern said. The “lowest hanging fruit,” according to multiple experts, is the PRESS Act , which would prevent the government from forcing journalists to reveal their sources and protect their notes from seizure. Dozens of states already have a version of the law, and at the national level, the PRESS Act passed the House unanimously in 2024. It had bipartisan support in the Senate but did not advance after Trump won the election and told Republicans to kill it. Trump and his administration have spent his second term cracking down on media leaks, resulting in multiple journalists being subpoenaed and at least one being raided. While Trump would almost certainly oppose another attempt at passing the PRESS Act, the midterms have left him with less political clout, potentially paving the way for the bill to gain enough support to override a veto. “Democrats should at least try, and if they can’t, it’ll be clear why they can’t,” Stern said. The crackdown on media leaks also makes reform of the Espionage Act important, according to Stern. One attempt at doing that is the Daniel Ellsberg Press Freedom and Whistleblower Protection Act , which would help protect whistleblowers and prevent the government from using the act to go after journalists and publishers. Stern pointed out that the Espionage Act has been used to go after politicians and government officials ranging from former FBI director James Comey to Trump himself, so support for reform should be bipartisan. Anti-SLAPP legislation is another area in which state versions already exist, setting the stage for a federal version. Forty states — including many Republican-led states — have laws prohibiting meritless lawsuits. Those laws help protect journalists who often face groundless defamation lawsuits meant to silence and punish them by forcing them to spend time and money to fight back in court. Since taking office, Trump himself has filed several defamation lawsuits against media organizations whose coverage he dislikes, including the BBC , The New York Times and The Wall Street Journal . That makes passing anti-SLAPP protections at the federal level imperative, press freedom advocates said. Finally, Congress could pass the Right to Record Act , which would create financial penalties if a federal officer tries to threaten or stop a person peacefully recording federal law enforcement activity. It would also punish the seizure or destruction of such recordings. “People on the ground covering what the Trump administration has done these last two years, where ICE and the National Guard and the military are being deployed — those journalists often come under the most aggressive assault, scrutiny and attempts to criminalize their activities,” said Free Press senior counsel Nora Benavidez. She pointed to the examples of independent journalists Don Lemon and Georgia Fort, who were arrested in January after they filmed and livestreamed from an anti-ICE protest at a Minnesota church. Numerous other journalists across the country have been assaulted and detained while covering anti-ICE demonstrations and immigration operations. Press freedom advocates acknowledged that Congress cannot legislate a solution to every First Amendment issue currently facing journalists. Congress is part of just one branch of the government, and it is the executive branch that “has used every tool at its disposal to try to censor and silence the press,” Zamora said. In addition to physical assaults on journalists by federal officers, Zamora pointed to the White House’s and Pentagon’s continued efforts to limit press access and the Department of Homeland Security’s attempt to revise visa rules to restrict foreign journalists working in the U.S. Trump’s ability to veto legislation will also be a major issue. Still, there is value in pushing an agenda supporting press freedom for the next two years, Weimers said. “If nothing else, it sets you up to do it when there’s a new administration.” “My hope is that we get back to a place where press freedom is a nonpartisan American value that we can all get behind. We strayed away from that,” Weimers said. “Most of these things used to be common sense for just about every American. And I still think they are pretty common sense for the average American member of the public or voter. “But politicians need to realize this is our First Amendment rights. This really matters, and we can’t take it for granted.” This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE Tags: 2026 midterms , Congress , House of Representatives , legislation , Midterms , press freedom , Press freedoms , Senate , Trump and press freedom Angela Fu Angela Fu is a reporter for Poynter. She can be reached at afu@poynter.org, on Signal at angelafu.74, on Bluesky @angelanfu.bsky.social and on Twitter @angelanfu. Angela Fu Newly-elected members of the 120th Congress gather for a group photo on the steps of the House of Representatives at the Capitol in Washington, Thursday, Nov. 12, 2026. (AP Photo/J. Scott Applewhite) Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom Congressional investigations could put the administration’s attacks on the press under scrutiny, while bipartisan legislation offers a path to reform November 13, 2026 Angela Fu Opinion | Longtime NBC Sports announcer Dan Hicks, dies suddenly. He was 64 The longtime announcer and host was a staple in sports broadcasting, known for his voice, easy manner and mentorship November 13, 2026 Tom Jones The local newsroom is disappearing. The news could pay the price. As newspapers abandon their buildings, they risk losing the mentoring, institutional knowledge and presence that help make good reporting possible November 13, 2026 Jackie Spinner Scott Pelley says CBS has lost its credibility. A post-midterms about-face wouldn’t help. ‘It’s not a question of which way the bias leans. The problem is that editorial control was taken from journalists’ November 12, 2026 Angela Fu Opinion | Face the changes: Two top leaders are leaving CBS News’ ‘Face the Nation’ The departures of two veteran editorial leaders give Bari Weiss an opening to reshape the influential Sunday political news program November 12, 2026 Tom Jones Back to News Comments Cancel reply You must be logged in to post a comment. This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. L