      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Nick Wright claims Clay Travis fabricates political beliefs for clicks",
      "pageDescription": "Nick Wright of &#039;First Things First&#039; joined Tim Miller on &#039;The Bulwark Daily&#039; and ripped his colleagues grifting with fake political personas.",
      "evidenceText": "Nick Wright claims Clay Travis fabricates political beliefs for clicks Skip to content Credit: The Bulwark, USA TODAY Network via Reuters Connect By Megan Armstrong on 11/12/2026 11/12/2026 Less AI and more AA in your search results. Nick Wright has become a sports media superstar on FS1’s First Things First and his own eponymous podcast, and as he recently explained to Andrew Marchand , his lifelong dream had always been to parlay his sports broadcasting fame into a career in politics. The past decade of American politics, Wright told Marchand, left him feeling “beaten down” and changed his mind about formally entering the political arena. However, in the aftermath of “ The Blue Tsunami ” in the midterm elections, Wright is slowly reclaiming his rightful place in political and social commentary. Wright called out Stephen A. Smith for his grift-adjacent persona as a fair-weather political pundit before delivering a 32-minute analysis of the midterms on What’s Wright? With Nick Wright and burying Matthew Yglesias for baseless critiques of newly elected Sen. Abdul El-Sayed (D-Mich.). On Wednesday, Tim Miller of The Bulwark welcomed Wright on his The Bulwark Daily podcast to “ lib out .” Ben Stiller loved it , but Clay Travis presumably did not. .@getnickwright tells @Timodc why a private admission from Clay Travis 11 years ago destroyed his respect for him: “If what you’re saying is true, that you don’t really believe what you’re saying, it’s just for audience and enrichment, I actually think that’s worse.” https://t.co/EPnCJq8eih — The Bulwark (@BulwarkOnline) November 12, 2026 “I’ve always been an unapologetic, you know, I’m a liberal Democrat,” Wright said. “And in my field, what is frustrating to me is—everyone’s allowed whatever politics they are. I really, really don’t like the folks who have the opposite politics of mine who pretend they’re centrists or independents. They’re a lot of folks in my space, the tell is, if they make a big mistake or a fact error or tell a story that’s not true accidentally, it’s always because it was fed from the right-wing ecosystem. Those guys who claim they’re down-the-middle neutral never tell a crazy, zany left-wing falsehood that they stumbled upon because all they’re stumbling upon is the right-wing crazy stuff.” Smartly, Miller asked whether Wright had anyone specific in mind. “One of my first TV things ever was with Clay,” Wright said. “And then we went to dinner after, and he told me, he was like, ‘You know I worked for Obama, and I just do this’ or whatever. Luckily, there was a third person at this dinner, so if he disputes it, I have a witness. I told him, I was like, ‘I want to be very clear. If what you’re saying is true—that you don’t really believe what you’re saying, it’s just for audience and enrichment, I actually think that’s worse, and it is impossible for me to ever respect you.’ And I left. That was the last time I ever talked to him. That was 11 years ago.” Late Wednesday night, Travis responded to Wright. “Eleven years ago I was still a Democrat so this entire story is completely made up,” Travis tweeted. “I guess this is what happens when you start voting Republican, people make up entire stories about you in their heads and share it with their far left wing friends.” Eleven years ago I was still a Democrat so this entire story is completely made up. I guess this is what happens when you start voting Republican, people make up entire stories about you in their heads and share it with their far left wing friends. — Clay Travis (@ClayTravis) November 12, 2026 Hopefully, the anonymous third person who attended the dinner breaks the tie, but no word yet. Travis founded OutKick in 2011 and sold it to Fox in 2021. This June, Travis officially departed OutKick , and by August, he launched Sport & State , for which he interviewed President Donald Trump in late September. He also hosts The Clay Travis Show , which airs on FS1, and The Clay Travis & Buck Sexton Show . This is not the first time Wright has called out Travis for fabricating his public persona. In June 2017, Travis and Wright got into it over the Black Lives Matter movement. Wright tweeted , “Here’s @ClayTravis when he’s not in character, performing for the masses. Careful who you call a liar, Clay. Folks keep receipts these days.” The tweet included two screenshots of emails from Travis: Here’s @ClayTravis when he’s not in character, performing for the masses. Careful who you call a liar, Clay. Folks keep receipts these days https://t.co/gmP4sizMV7 — nick wright (@getnickwright) June 4, 2017 Here’s the year old tweet that references @ClayTravis that he seems to be so bothered by at the moment. https://t.co/0OhcpVtdrY — nick wright (@getnickwright) June 4, 2017 Clay is now saying that my tweet is a “lie”. Here’s some photographic evidence, which evidently he yearns for in all cases. https://t.co/QKx7HAzhaQ — nick wright (@getnickwright) June 4, 2017 On The Bulwark Daily , Wright didn’t stop at Travis. “Listen, the biggest name in my space, Stephen A., has more than flirted with politics,” Wright said. “And I just would like him to actually be honest with himself and the audience about what his politics are, and so be it.” “That he’s pro-Trump, you mean?” Miller interjected. “Yeah, I mean, pretty obviously,” Wright said. “And so, pretending like, oh, I’m just calling balls and strikes, like, no you’re not. In my field, the job is not to be unbiased. The job, however, is to be honest with your audience about your biases, and then let them call it how they see it.” Miller asked Wright to estimate “how many of those guys are faking it,” and while Wright didn’t keep naming names or put a specific number to it, he expertly demystified the grift. “It is so much easier to get an audience and make money [by] appealing to the lowest common denominator of the MAGA-sphere than it is to do it in sports,” Wright said. “Like, I’m going to sound like an a**hole here, but actually achieving at a high level in daily sports talk, it’s a very competitive space. And you have to get lucky, but you also really gotta be on it. “And so, I think it’s a lot of folks who are like I wasn’t good enough. I wanted to do commentary about X—pop culture, entertainment, sports—I’m not good enough at that. But I’m comfortable talking into a camera or a microphone, and I can con these people. I do think there’s an element of that.” Last week, Awful Announcing’s very own Sean Keeley pondered whether the historic Democratic victories in the midterms forecast “ a sports media blue wave .” If that indeed comes to fruition, Wright won’t be alone in poking holes in the red MAGA balloon that has hovered over media and society for 10-plus years. About Megan Armstrong Since first getting published as a student by the Kansas City Star in 2013, Megan Armstrong has gone wherever curiosity and stories have taken her, specializing in sports, media, music, and culture coverage. She also has an extensive background in interviewing and profiling public figures. Her work has appeared in Billboard, Boardroom, Bleacher Report, Esquire, GQ, GRAMMY.com, NYLON, Newsweek, Teen Vogue, The Hollywood Reporter, UPROXX, and elsewhere. View all posts by Megan Armstrong Related Content Ranking all of Taylor Swift’s songs about Travis Kelce Corned beef, Cheerwine, and carving stations: Ranking every NHL team’s media meals How to fix the ManningCast The top five candidates to replace Ernie Johnson on ‘Inside the NBA’ Recent Posts Brett Yormark is leaving the Big 12 to run a new venture for Redbird Capital built around their ownership of AC Milan. The NHL is ready to come to the negotiating table to renew its rights with ESPN and TNT at an increase over its current deal. Credit: ‘Run It Back’ on FanDuel TV \"You gotta be kidding me!\" 'When We Were Young and Fearless' examines how Kevin Durant, Russell Westbrook, and James Harden changed the NBA. \"He was genial. He was compassionate. He was great company, had a world-class sense of humor.\" Sidebar Popular Posts Five NFL teams play three consecutive primetime games this season; zero did so last year. Mike Florio thinks he knows why Where have you gone, Lee Corso? College football fans falling out of love with Saturday pregame shows Recent Posts Brett Yormark is leaving the Big 12 to run a new venture for Redbird Capital built around their ownership of AC Milan. The NHL is ready to come to the negotiating table to renew its rights with ESPN and TNT at an increase over its current deal. Credit: ‘Run It Back’ on FanDuel TV \"You gotta be kidding me!\" 'When We Were Young and Fearless' examines how Kevin Durant, Russell Westbrook, and James Harden changed the NBA. \"He was genial. He was compassionate. He was great company, had a world-class sense of humor.\"",
