      "id": "90b50bddfd9b477604a488ae2b1141a0fba6111ea6d3a9dd642119f7a040d0c3",
      "title": "Briefing: A new tool to explore satellite imagery",
      "url": "https://benjaminstrick.com/course",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T18:31:33.655Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://benjaminstrick.com/course",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "OSINT Course Online: Open Source Investigations | Benjamin Strick",
      "pageDescription": "Learn open source investigations with Benjamin Strick. Verify images, geolocate footage, use satellite imagery and complete your own investigation. £299.",
      "evidenceText": "OSINT Course Online: Open Source Investigations | Benjamin Strick Skip to content Watch the course preview 2 min 9 sec Open the course preview (2 min 9 sec) ≈ Approximately 14 Hours of video tutorials 72 Lessons 11 Modules Alongside the self-paced video tutorials, allow 4–6 hours for practical investigation work, including a full six-image pattern investigation. Turn an online claim into a finding you can support. For journalists, OSINT investigators, researchers and the digitally curious. Open source intelligence (OSINT) is the craft of finding, verifying and explaining public information. In this course I show you how I do it on real investigations. Then you try it yourself and compare your reasoning with my walkthrough. Learn where to start, what to check next and how to explain what you’ve found. What you’ll learn Real clues. Your next question. Work out where and when something happened, follow the links between sources and keep a record another investigator can check. GEOLOCATION & CHRONOLOCATION Where and when did this happen? Use visual analysis to spot clues in photos, videos and satellite imagery. Match landmarks and road layouts to maps to test a location. Compare shadows and changes in the scene with dated sources to narrow down when it happened. MOVEMENT & IDENTITY What can you connect? Trace aircraft and ships through ownership and sanctions records. I’ll show you how to write up your findings in verification notes and illustrate the connections with annotated visuals and maps. RUNNING AN INVESTIGATION How do you run an investigation? Set up a safe research workspace, protect privacy and use an investigation checklist to plan your checks. Build a simple evidence database so you can trace your findings and a teammate can review or continue your work. Inside the course From your first find to a full investigation. A guided path through the methods, with an independent investigation to bring them together. Watch. Try. Review. Follow the demonstration, make your own attempt, then use the walkthrough to review and revise your findings. Modules 0–10 Expand all 00 Start here + Turn curiosity into an investigation question, make your first find and save a record you can return to. 01 Safety and ethics + Build a proportionate research setup, use researcher accounts responsibly, protect privacy and consider potential harm before you begin. 02 Search and collect + Search across websites, platforms and languages. Recover changed pages, preserve useful material and keep a research trail you can follow. 03 Verify images and video + Separate the claims, examine context and metadata, reverse-search images, trace source history and assess possible manipulation or synthetic content. 04 Geolocation + Read visible clues, develop candidate locations and test them against maps and reference imagery. Check AI-generated leads and report the location the evidence supports. 05 Chronolocation + Investigate when something happened using reporting, visual clues, sun position, satellite imagery and fire-detection records. Keep upload time and event time separate. 06 Satellite imagery and change detection + Choose imagery for a question, connect located views and compare a site over time. Combine the sources into a finding that makes their limits clear. 07 Movement, asset identity and roles + Research aircraft and vessels through visible features, registrations, movement records and ownership information. Distinguish the object, its owner and its operator. 08 Writing and presenting your findings + Write clear findings, make explanatory graphics and use maps to help someone else understand your evidence and reasoning. 09 Your independent investigation + Investigate a set of photographs and a caption independently, then use the visual walkthrough and review criteria to revise your findings. 10 What next? + Explore subjects you could investigate, ways to share useful work and settings where these skills can contribute. Your instructor “The course I wish I had when I started. ” Over the past decade, I’ve trained thousands of people to use open source intelligence techniques, from checking a claim to investigating events using multiple sources. I’ve worked with international news organisations and NGOs to investigate conflict, grow innovative OSINT techniques, document wrongdoing and challenge disinformation, combining good analysis with automation to work at scale. Benjamin Strick · Open source investigator and educator Put the methods into practice Build your own investigation. Leave with an organised research record, annotated comparisons and findings you’ve reviewed and revised. A process of analysis, OSINT techniques and methods you can return to as the tools change. Clear research notes Visual evidence Findings you can explain The Art of Open Source Analysis About 14 hours of video tutorials across 72 lessons in 11 modules Manage an investigation by planning your checks, keeping a source log and writing up your findings Practical exercises and research materials Workbook and editable worksheets Independent investigation and walkthrough Guidance on writing, graphics and maps £299 Shown in your local currency at checkout. Local sales tax may be added at checkout. Enrol in the course ↗ Enrolment and payment through Thinkific. Full refund within 14 days if you’ve opened fewer than 20% of the lessons. Terms apply. Terms of Use · Privacy Policy Group orders If you’re buying course places for colleagues or students, tell me how many people you’d like to enrol. Enquire about a bulk order ↓ The Art of Open Source Analysis Group order enquiry If you’re buying places for colleagues or students, tell me a little about your group. I’ll reply by email with pricing and next steps. Close × Your email Organisation (optional) Number of places Anything else? (optional) Team enquiry privacy I only use what you send here to reply about places for your group. Formspree passes your enquiry to the course inbox (Gmail), and Cloudflare Turnstile checks it isn’t spam. I delete enquiries 12 months after my last reply, unless they lead to an order. To see or delete yours, email [email protected] . See the Privacy Policy for who handles your data and your rights, and Cloudflare’s Turnstile Privacy Addendum . Send enquiry → The Art of Open Source Analysis Thanks for your team enquiry I’ll reply by email with pricing and next steps for your group. Ben In the meantime, here’s some of my recent work. A few practical questions Before you get started. Can I do this course whenever? + Absolutely. The course is self-paced, so you can start when it suits you and work through the lessons at your own speed. Pause after a module, come back to an exercise later or rewatch a lesson when you need it. Thinkific saves your progress as you go. How long do I have access? + For as long as the course is offered on the site. If it’s ever withdrawn, you’ll get at least 30 days’ notice by email so you can finish it. You can watch in a web browser or the Thinkific mobile app. Do I need previous experience? + No previous OSINT experience is needed. We start with a question, a first find and a simple record. If you already know some tools, the curriculum will help you judge how much is new to you. How is this different from OSINT At Home? + OSINT At Home is my free YouTube series. Each video covers one technique. This course puts the methods in order, adds exercises, worksheets and real cases, and ends with an independent investigation you review against my walkthrough. If you’ve watched the videos and wondered what to do next, this is it. What equipment and software do I need? + A laptop or desktop, a reliable internet connection and somewhere to keep your notes. Many core methods use free tools and public information. Some services require accounts or charge for particular features or historical data. Will my exercises be marked or made public? + You can keep your work private. The course uses practical exercises, walkthroughs and self-review, rather than individual grading. Save your first attempt, then review and revise it. Is there a certificate? + Yes. You’ll receive a certificate of completion when you finish the course. It shows you completed the lessons. The course uses self-review rather than individual grading, so it isn’t an accredited qualification. Does the course include difficult footage? + Some lessons use conflict and human rights material that may be distressing. Researcher safety and managing exposure are part of the course. Take breaks and use alternative exercises where provided.",
