      "pageTitle": "Longtime NBC Sports commentator Dan Hicks passes away at 64",
      "pageDescription": "Longtime NBC Sports commentator Dan Hicks passed away suddenly from an &quot;acute illness&quot; at age 64, it was announced on Thursday.",
      "evidenceText": "Longtime NBC Sports commentator Dan Hicks passes away at 64 Skip to content Credit: USA Today Network Pictures Archive By Drew Lerner on 11/12/2026 Less AI and more AA in your search results. The sports media world was sent into a state of shock on Thursday morning as longtime NBC Sports commentator Dan Hicks passed away at 64. Prior to his sudden passing, Hicks served as the lead play-by-play voice of NBC Sports’ PGA Tour coverage, returned as the lead voice of Notre Dame football in 2024, and was also the voice of several prominent Olympic sports for NBC including swimming and skiing. He spent over three decades at NBC after beginning his national TV career as a sports reporter at CNN. Hicks was married to veteran ESPN broadcaster Hannah Storm. Statements on Hicks’ devastating passing poured out on Thursday morning, beginning with his wife. https://t.co/8CK7OLXbZx — Hannah Storm (@HannahStormESPN) November 12, 2026 “Our family is heartbroken by the sudden and unexpected passing of Dan following an acute illness. He was a devoted husband over 30 years, loving father to our three daughters, and cherished friend to so many. His loss is immeasurable,” Storm said in a statement. “Dan was truly one of a kind and, quite simply, the person everyone wanted to be around. When he walked into a room, he brought the energy, the laughter and the people together. He was endlessly kind, thoughtful and funny, and he made everyone feel like they mattered. Our three daughters and I are heartbroken. We are grateful for the love and support surrounding our family during this incredibly difficult time. There are simply no words to express how much he will be missed.” Rich Lerner, Hicks’ longtime colleague at NBC Sports and Golf Channel, announced the news on Golf Channel on Thursday morning. Rich Lerner announces on Golf Channel that longtime NBC Sports broadcaster Dan Hicks has passed away at 64. Hicks was the longtime lead play-by-play voice of the PGA Tour, Notre Dame football, and Olympic swimming and skiing on NBC. https://t.co/45HBapuMhg — Awful Announcing (@awfulannouncing) November 12, 2026 Hicks called any number of legendary moments throughout his career. Among them was his memorable call on the 72nd hole of the 2008 U.S. Open, when Tiger Woods rolled in a 12-foot birdie putt to tie Rocco Mediate and force a playoff the next day. “Expect anything different!?” Hicks bellowed in a call that would live on forever. The “EXPECT ANYTHING DIFFERENT?” call from Dan Hicks after the Tiger birdie on the 72nd hole at 2008 US Open at Torrey to force a playoff with Rocco Mediate never gets old🔥 As the US Open resumes at Oakmont, appreciate the greatness of @DanHicksNBC 🫡 https://t.co/6zEdHNZAdl — Jack Benjamin (@JackBenjaminPxP) June 15, 2025 Hicks was also the voice of Michael Phelps’ legendary Olympic run in 2008 when the swimmer won a record-setting eight gold medals. On the winter side of things, Hicks voiced Lindsey Vonn’s dominant run in alpine skiing. NBC Sports president Rick Cordella, and executive producers Sam Flood and Molly Solomon also released a statement on Thursday. https://t.co/nEscSk889a — NBC Sports PR (@NBCSportsPR) November 12, 2026 The PGA Tour also released a statement mourning Hicks. https://t.co/Uy2Gm3FFxi — PGA Tour (@PGATour) November 12, 2026 The outpouring of support among former colleagues has been heavy and swift. Golf Channel remains in breaking news coverage about Hicks’ passing at the time of this writing. RIP. About Drew Lerner Drew Lerner is a staff writer for Awful Announcing and an aspiring cable subscriber. He previously covered sports media for Sports Media Watch. Future beat writer for the Oasis reunion tour. Drew writes the Awful Announcing 'The A Block' newsletter every Friday Subscribe here . View all posts by Drew Lerner Related Content Corned beef, Cheerwine, and carving stations: Ranking every NHL team’s media meals Where have you gone, Lee Corso? College football fans falling out of love with Saturday pregame shows Five players who deserved to win the Heisman Trophy but didn’t Why Joe Buck and Troy Aikman is the most painful breakup in sports media history Recent Posts Washington Commanders quarterback Sam Hartman will follow in the footsteps of Jesse Palmer in starring as 'The Bachelor.' Brett Yormark is leaving the Big 12 to run a new venture for Redbird Capital built around their ownership of AC Milan. The NHL is ready to come to the negotiating table to renew its rights with ESPN and TNT at an increase over its current deal. Credit: ‘Run It Back’ on FanDuel TV \"You gotta be kidding me!\" 'When We Were Young and Fearless' examines how Kevin Durant, Russell Westbrook, and James Harden changed the NBA. Sidebar Popular Posts The 76ers are either building a superteam or creating a superproblem Is a sports media blue wave coming, too? Recent Posts Washington Commanders quarterback Sam Hartman will follow in the footsteps of Jesse Palmer in starring as 'The Bachelor.' Brett Yormark is leaving the Big 12 to run a new venture for Redbird Capital built around their ownership of AC Milan. The NHL is ready to come to the negotiating table to renew its rights with ESPN and TNT at an increase over its current deal. Credit: ‘Run It Back’ on FanDuel TV \"You gotta be kidding me!\" 'When We Were Young and Fearless' examines how Kevin Durant, Russell Westbrook, and James Harden changed the NBA.",
